================================================================================
W1B  the P3 dressed amplitude [R]: boundable at beta=2.5, or the binding input?
================================================================================

[1] exact two-sided PIN  0 <= f1^2 <= M0 = integral dmu   [T]
    (M0 = sum_n f_n^2 >= f1^2 since all f_n^2 >= 0, f1^2 is one term)
    At beta=0 the two exact anchors hit BOTH ends of the pin:
      beta=0 :  f1^2=a0=0.06250   M0=0.25000   f1^2/M0=0.250   (SU(2) real fund.: M0=1/N^2 -> w1=s/4=0.250)
      beta=0 :  f1^2=a0=0.02778   M0=0.05556   f1^2/M0=0.500   (N=3 complex fund.: M0=1/(2N^2) -> w1=s/2=0.500)
      beta=0 :  f1^2=a0=0.01000   M0=0.02000   f1^2/M0=0.500   (N=5 complex fund.: M0=1/(2N^2) -> w1=s/2=0.500)
      beta=0 :  f1^2=a0=0.00250   M0=0.00500   f1^2/M0=0.500   (N=10 complex fund.: M0=1/(2N^2) -> w1=s/2=0.500)
    => strong-coupling anchor: f1^2 sits at the MIDPOINT (w1=1/2) of [0,M0].
    => 2D/free anchor: f1^2=0 sits at the BOTTOM of [0,M0] (W3: w1=0 exactly).
    Both satisfy every RP/KL/variance/gauge axiom  =>  the pin cannot fix the SIGN.
    beta=2.5 one-plaquette proxy pin of  f1^2/a0  (a0=s/(4N^2), s=1):
      SU(2): M0(2.5)=0.13412  a0=0.06250  ->  f1^2/a0 in [0, 2.146]
      SU(3): M0(2.5)=0.05242  a0=0.02778  ->  f1^2/a0 in [0, 1.887]
    the window [0,~1.8] contains BOTH  f1^2=0  and  f1^2~a0  -> VACUOUS for the sign.

[2] KP radius u*K<1 :  the only proven bound |log D_fin| <= C u  [T]/[NG]
    (P3-DRESSED-MASS 4.1: C = Ursell constant, finite iff u*K<1)
    K(beta) = 80 e^{2+beta} N  (PROOF-P3 3, beta-dependent);  u=c_f/N
      SU(2): c_f(2.5)=2.0423  u=1.0212  K=1.440e+04  u*K=1.47e+04  -> OUTSIDE (bound vacuous)
      SU(3): c_f(2.5)=10.6379  u=3.5460  K=2.160e+04  u*K=7.66e+04  -> OUTSIDE (bound vacuous)
      SU(2): small-beta KP boundary u*K=1 at beta_KP=0.0067  (2.5 is 372x beyond)
      SU(3): small-beta KP boundary u*K=1 at beta_KP=0.0100  (2.5 is 249x beyond)
    => the convergent constant C (and any O(1) bound on c) lives only on beta<~0.01-0.02;
       at 2.5 the decoration gas is outside the Kotecky-Preiss disk. No bound on c.

[3] RP consequences are UPPER-BOUND shaped: they hold at f1^2=0 too  [T]/[V]
      atom  (f1^2>0)     min(d2 logC)=2.58e-14 (>0 convex?)   m_eff decreasing? YES   m_eff(end)=0.800
      pure cut (f1^2=0)  min(d2 logC)=6.90e-08 (>0 convex?)   m_eff decreasing? YES   m_eff(end)=3.366
    both pass RP's consequences => RP cannot tell f1^2>0 from f1^2=0  (as in T2 2.1).

[4] [R] and W differ ONLY by the normaliser M0  [T]
    W : w1 = f1^2/M0 >= w0         [R] : f1^2 >= delta a0
    identity: w1 = (f1^2/a0) * (a0/M0).  a0/M0 is EXACT and >0 (one-plaquette).
      SU(3) beta=2.5: a0/M0 = 0.5299  =>  w1 = 0.5299 * (f1^2/a0)
    so SIGN(w1) = SIGN(f1^2) = SIGN(1 - c u + ...):  the SAME object.
    W and [R] are equivalent iff 0 < inf M0 <= sup M0 < inf  (variance bounded);
    M0 is itself a positivity datum (W1: not the obstruction). => [R] IS W.

[5] five acid tests for the amplitude object f1^2 = a0(1 - c u + ...):
    (1) free massless: no binding f(x)fbar->1 recoupling => a0=0 => f1^2=0  [T]
    (2) 2D: <chi_{1/2}|chi_{3/2}>=1.39e-17 => f1^2=0 (C2(R) x orthogonality)  [T]/[V]
    (3) one plaquette: atom DEGENERATE, a0/M0=s/2 is an artefact (no t-direction)  [T]
    (4) strong coupling: w1(0)=s/2 (N>=3), s/4 (SU(2)) reproduced  [T] (mod KL)
    (5) large N: a0=1/(4N^2), M0=1/(2N^2) both O(N^-2) => w1=O(N^0), N-uniform  [T]
    (6) continuum: f1^2->Z_O^2 f1^2, M0->Z_O^2 M0 => w1 invariant (gamma_w1=0)  [T]

